GRAHAM SEQUENCE (STRUCTURE)
============================================================
511.33 — Large numbers / combinatorics-related number theory
Definition:
    g(1)   = 3 ↑↑↑↑ 3
    g(n+1) = 3 ↑^(g(n)) 3

Meaning:
    ↑^k    = k-arrow hyperoperator
    g(64)  = Graham's Number

------------------------------------------------------------
                 EXPANSION STRUCTURE (NOT VALUES)
------------------------------------------------------------

g(1):
    3 ↑↑↑↑ 3
    = hyperop_4(3,3)

g(2):
    3 ↑^(g(1)) 3
    = hyperop_(g(1))(3,3)

g(3):
    3 ↑^(g(2)) 3
    = hyperop_(g(2))(3,3)

...
...
...

g(64):
    3 ↑^(g(63)) 3
    = hyperop_(g(63))(3,3)

------------------------------------------------------------
                 OPERATOR-LEVEL RECURSION
------------------------------------------------------------

Let:
    H(k, a, b) = a ↑^k b

Then:
    g(1)   = H(4, 3, 3)
    g(n+1) = H(g(n), 3, 3)

------------------------------------------------------------
                 HYPEROPERATOR LADDER (ASCII)
------------------------------------------------------------

    ↑^1 : exponentiation
        a ↑ b = a^b

    ↑^2 : tetration
        a ↑↑ b = a^(a^(...^a))   (b times)

    ↑^3 : pentation
        a ↑↑↑ b = hyper-tetration

    ↑^4 : hexation
        a ↑↑↑↑ b = hyper-pentation

    ↑^k : k-hyperoperation
        a ↑^k b = H(k, a, b)

------------------------------------------------------------
                 GRAHAM'S NUMBER (ASCII)
------------------------------------------------------------

G = g(64)

Where:
    g(64) = H(g(63), 3, 3)
    g(63) = H(g(62), 3, 3)
    ...
    g(2)  = H(g(1), 3, 3)
    g(1)  = H(4, 3, 3)

------------------------------------------------------------
                 MODULAR FACT (LAST DIGITS)
------------------------------------------------------------

G mod 10^12 = 7262464195387

